    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Observability when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Observability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally. A concrete application is Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Observability provides the required behavior: Observability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Observability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Observability fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Observability when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Observability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally. A concrete application is Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Observability provides the required behavior: Observability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Observability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Observability fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
