    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about Architecture principles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nArchitecture principles are reusable decision guidelines covering boundaries, dependencies, quality attributes, and evolution.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Architecture principles accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Architecture principles are reusable decision guidelines covering boundaries, dependencies, quality attributes, and evolution.\n2. Use a concrete case: A principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Architecture principles first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Architecture principles when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Architecture principles, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
