    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Architecture principles is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Architecture principles are reusable decision guidelines covering boundaries, dependencies, quality attributes, and evolution.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Architecture principles are reusable decision guidelines covering boundaries, dependencies, quality attributes, and evolution.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Architecture principles when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a principle such as explicit ownership can guide design choices when multiple services need to access the same business data. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
