    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Trade-offs in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Trade-offs are the deliberate balance among competing goals such as latency, consistency, cost, complexity, and reliability.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Trade-offs, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Trade-offs when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Trade-offs as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Trade-offs.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
