    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Trade-offs when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Trade-offs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Trade-offs are the deliberate balance among competing goals such as latency, consistency, cost, complexity, and reliability.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Trade-offs, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Trade-offs when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a design decision should state what capability improves, what cost or complexity is introduced, and why that balance is acceptable for the workload. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Trade-offs as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Trade-offs.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
