    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen DDD architecture misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to DDD architecture. DDD architecture uses domain boundaries and domain models to keep business rules explicit while isolating infrastructure concerns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls DDD architecture: DDD architecture uses domain boundaries and domain models to keep business rules explicit while isolating infrastructure concerns.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Bounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nBounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use DDD architecture when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, bounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
