    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain DDD architecture in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Bounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: DDD architecture uses domain boundaries and domain models to keep business rules explicit while isolating infrastructure concerns.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Bounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain.\n4. Finish with the limitation: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nBounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use DDD architecture when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, bounded contexts can keep different business models separate when the same word means different things in different parts of a domain. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
