    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about Service boundaries.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nService boundaries define which responsibilities and data belong together and where independently deployable components communicate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Service boundaries accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Service boundaries define which responsibilities and data belong together and where independently deployable components communicate.\n2. Use a concrete case: A service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Service boundaries first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Service boundaries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Service boundaries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Service boundaries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Service boundaries, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
