    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Service boundaries misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Service boundaries. Service boundaries define which responsibilities and data belong together and where independently deployable components communicate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Service boundaries: Service boundaries define which responsibilities and data belong together and where independently deployable components communicate.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Service boundaries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Service boundaries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Service boundaries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
