    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you change your Service boundaries design when traffic, failure rate, or organizational constraints change?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Service boundaries.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Service boundaries define which responsibilities and data belong together and where independently deployable components communicate.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Service boundaries when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service should own a coherent business capability and its data so changes do not require synchronized deployments across unrelated components. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Service boundaries as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Service boundaries.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
