    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDistributed data is data stored across multiple nodes or regions, requiring explicit choices around partitioning, replication, consistency, and failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Distributed data accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Distributed data is data stored across multiple nodes or regions, requiring explicit choices around partitioning, replication, consistency, and failure.\n2. Use a concrete case: Choose a consistency model, ownership model, and failure strategy before spreading one data set across multiple services or regions.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Concurrency or distribution can improve throughput and resilience, but it introduces coordination, ordering, failure, and debugging concerns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Distributed data first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nChoose a consistency model, ownership model, and failure strategy before spreading one data set across multiple services or regions.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Distributed data when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, choose a consistency model, ownership model, and failure strategy before spreading one data set across multiple services or regions. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Distributed data, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
