    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about High availability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHigh availability uses redundancy and failure handling to reduce service downtime within a defined failure model.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define High availability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: High availability uses redundancy and failure handling to reduce service downtime within a defined failure model.\n2. Use a concrete case: Remove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For High availability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define High availability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nRemove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use High availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, remove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor High availability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
