    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about High availability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen High availability misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to High availability. High availability uses redundancy and failure handling to reduce service downtime within a defined failure model.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls High availability: High availability uses redundancy and failure handling to reduce service downtime within a defined failure model.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Remove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For High availability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nRemove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use High availability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, remove or contain single points of failure and test the failover path instead of assuming redundancy alone guarantees availability. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
