    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because disaster recovery prepares technology and operational procedures to restore service and data after a major outage. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how DR addresses it: Disaster recovery prepares technology and operational procedures to restore service and data after a major outage.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For DR, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use DR when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating DR as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with DR.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
