    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about DR.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen DR misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to DR. Disaster recovery prepares technology and operational procedures to restore service and data after a major outage.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls DR: Disaster recovery prepares technology and operational procedures to restore service and data after a major outage.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For DR, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use DR when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating DR as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with DR.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
