    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about DR.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDR is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For DR, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what DR gives you: Disaster recovery prepares technology and operational procedures to restore service and data after a major outage.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For DR, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use DR when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a disaster-recovery design should be tested through restore and failover exercises so the stated RTO and RPO are more than documentation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating DR as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with DR.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
