    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about Event architecture.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEvent architecture uses events to communicate state changes between components while decoupling producers from consumers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Event architecture accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Event architecture uses events to communicate state changes between components while decoupling producers from consumers.\n2. Use a concrete case: Publish domain events when there is a useful business fact for other components to consume, and define delivery, ordering, and compatibility expectations.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Event architecture first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPublish domain events when there is a useful business fact for other components to consume, and define delivery, ordering, and compatibility expectations.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Event architecture when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, publish domain events when there is a useful business fact for other components to consume, and define delivery, ordering, and compatibility expectations. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Event architecture, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
