    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI governance establishes conventions and controls for design, versioning, security, compatibility, and lifecycle management.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define API governance accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: API governance establishes conventions and controls for design, versioning, security, compatibility, and lifecycle management.\n2. Use a concrete case: Define consistent API conventions for authentication, errors, versioning, naming, and lifecycle so many teams can integrate without inventing incompatible rules.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For API governance, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define API governance first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nDefine consistent API conventions for authentication, errors, versioning, naming, and lifecycle so many teams can integrate without inventing incompatible rules.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use API governance when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, define consistent API conventions for authentication, errors, versioning, naming, and lifecycle so many teams can integrate without inventing incompatible rules. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor API governance, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
