    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about Zero Trust.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Zero Trust misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Zero Trust. Zero Trust treats trust as something to verify continuously, with explicit authentication, authorization, and least-privilege controls.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Zero Trust: Zero Trust treats trust as something to verify continuously, with explicit authentication, authorization, and least-privilege controls.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A service-to-service request should be authenticated and authorized based on identity and policy rather than trusted solely because it came from an internal network.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA service-to-service request should be authenticated and authorized based on identity and policy rather than trusted solely because it came from an internal network.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Zero Trust when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service-to-service request should be authenticated and authorized based on identity and policy rather than trusted solely because it came from an internal network. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
