    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Modernization when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Modernization improves or replaces legacy technology while preserving required business capability and controlling migration risk. A concrete application is Modernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Modernization provides the required behavior: Modernization improves or replaces legacy technology while preserving required business capability and controlling migration risk.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: Modernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Modernization, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Modernization fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nModernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Modernization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, modernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Modernization as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Modernization.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
