    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Modernization when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Modernization.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Modernization improves or replaces legacy technology while preserving required business capability and controlling migration risk.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Modernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Modernization, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nModernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use Modernization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, modernize the part of the system where change is constrained by real pain such as reliability, cost, security exposure, or delivery speed rather than replacing technology for its own sake. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Modernization as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Modernization.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
