    slug: "software-architecture-10-years-of-expirance-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-adrs-198",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software Architecture 10+ Years of expirance interview question about ADRs.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nADRs is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For ADRs, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what ADRs gives you: Architecture Decision Records capture important technical decisions, context, alternatives, and consequences so teams can understand why a system looks the way it does.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For ADRs, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An ADR can record why a team chose asynchronous messaging over synchronous calls, including the constraints and consequences that led to the decision.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn ADR can record why a team chose asynchronous messaging over synchronous calls, including the constraints and consequences that led to the decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA platform-scale architecture may use ADRs when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an ADR can record why a team chose asynchronous messaging over synchronous calls, including the constraints and consequences that led to the decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ADRs as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ADRs.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
