    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Connection pools when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Connection pools.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Connection pools keep a bounded set of reusable database connections so requests avoid creating a new connection for every operation.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A database connection pool reuses established connections and limits the number of concurrent connections; pool size should match database and application capacity rather than simply being made large.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Connection pools, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA database connection pool reuses established connections and limits the number of concurrent connections; pool size should match database and application capacity rather than simply being made large.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Connection pools when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a database connection pool reuses established connections and limits the number of concurrent connections; pool size should match database and application capacity rather than simply being made large. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Connection pools as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Connection pools.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
