    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTransactions group related database changes so the required atomicity, consistency, and isolation rules are maintained.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Transactions accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Transactions group related database changes so the required atomicity, consistency, and isolation rules are maintained.\n2. Use a concrete case: An order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Transactions, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Transactions first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Transactions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an order workflow can update related records in one database transaction when those writes must either all commit or all roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Transactions, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
