    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about Isolation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTransaction isolation controls which effects of concurrent transactions are visible to one another.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Isolation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Transaction isolation controls which effects of concurrent transactions are visible to one another.\n2. Use a concrete case: A transaction isolation level determines which concurrent changes a transaction can observe; stronger isolation can reduce anomalies at the cost of concurrency or contention.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Isolation, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Isolation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA transaction isolation level determines which concurrent changes a transaction can observe; stronger isolation can reduce anomalies at the cost of concurrency or contention.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Isolation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a transaction isolation level determines which concurrent changes a transaction can observe; stronger isolation can reduce anomalies at the cost of concurrency or contention. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Isolation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
