    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nObject-relational mapping translates between application objects and relational data models, handling persistence mechanics while introducing mapping and query considerations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define ORM accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Object-relational mapping translates between application objects and relational data models, handling persistence mechanics while introducing mapping and query considerations.\n2. Use a concrete case: An ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For ORM, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define ORM first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use ORM when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor ORM, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
