    slug: "backend-engineer-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-orm-68",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about ORM.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nORM is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For ORM, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what ORM gives you: Object-relational mapping translates between application objects and relational data models, handling persistence mechanics while introducing mapping and query considerations.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For ORM, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use ORM when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an ORM maps application objects to relational data and can reduce repetitive SQL, but engineers still need to understand generated queries and transaction behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
