    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about N+1.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define N+1 accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n2. Use a concrete case: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define N+1 first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor N+1, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
