    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for N+1 is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
