    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of N+1 comes from the behavior it provides: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n3. State the trade-off: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
