    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about N+1.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen N+1 misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to N+1. The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls N+1: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
