    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nN+1 is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what N+1 gives you: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nN+1 is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what N+1 gives you: The N+1 query problem occurs when one query loads a set of records and then an additional query runs for each record, creating avoidable database round trips.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Batching or joining related data can reduce round trips, but fetching too much data at once can increase row volume, memory use, or coupling between queries.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use N+1 when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if loading 100 orders causes one query for orders plus 100 customer queries, an ORM can be producing an N+1 pattern; inspect SQL and use an appropriate fetch strategy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
