    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about Indexes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIndexes provide searchable structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write maintenance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Indexes accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Indexes provide searchable structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write maintenance.\n2. Use a concrete case: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Indexes first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Indexes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Indexes, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
