    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Indexes are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Indexes provide searchable structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write maintenance.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nAn index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Indexes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Indexes. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
