    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Query plans is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A query plan describes how a database intends to execute a query, including access paths, joins, and estimated work.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A query plan describes how a database intends to execute a query, including access paths, joins, and estimated work.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: An execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nAn execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Query plans when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
