    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuery plans is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Query plans gives you: A query plan describes how a database intends to execute a query, including access paths, joins, and estimated work.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Query plans when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an execution plan shows how the database expects to access rows, joins, and indexes; compare it with actual runtime behavior when diagnosing a slow query. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
