    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Caching addresses it: Caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
