    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Caching misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Caching. Caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Caching: Caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
