    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Caching in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Caching stores reusable results closer to the caller to reduce repeated work or latency, while requiring an explicit freshness and invalidation strategy.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n4. Finish with the limitation: The gain in speed or load reduction has to be balanced against stale data, invalidation complexity, and memory use.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
