    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRate limiting bounds how many requests an identity or resource may make over time to protect capacity and reduce abuse.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Rate limiting accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Rate limiting bounds how many requests an identity or resource may make over time to protect capacity and reduce abuse.\n2. Use a concrete case: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Rate limiting, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Rate limiting first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nLimit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Rate limiting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Rate limiting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Rate limiting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Rate limiting, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
