    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about Authentication.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAuthentication is a production engineering concern that should be understood in terms of its behavior, boundaries, and trade-offs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Authentication accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Authentication is a production engineering concern that should be understood in terms of its behavior, boundaries, and trade-offs.\n2. Use a concrete case: A service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Authentication first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Authentication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Authentication, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
