    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPagination is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Pagination, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Pagination gives you: Pagination divides large result sets into smaller responses using a defined cursor or page scheme.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Pagination, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Pagination when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pagination as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pagination.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
