    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Backend Engineer interview question about Observability.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nObservability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Observability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Observability uses signals such as logs, metrics, and traces to infer what a production system is doing internally.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Observability, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Observability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA backend service may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Observability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
